---
source: tests/golden.rs
expression: report
input_file: tests/fixtures/handwritten-trusted-denial.log
---
--- rules exit 0 ---
# rulesteward: line 19: no trust= in this record, so the trust-vs-rule decision has no input; a scoped rule never widens global trust, so a rule is the safe default
# rulesteward: line 21: path is unrepresentable in a rule (space or colon; the rule parser has no quoting and a colon flips the format), and the file is already trusted, so there is nothing safe to emit
# rulesteward: line 22: trust attribute unavailable (trust=?); emitting nothing, because the file's trust state is unknown rather than untrusted
# rulesteward: new file: none recommended (rules.d/ not read: --no-conf, legacy fapolicyd.rules, or unreadable)
# rulesteward: 1 untrusted path(s) need a trust entry, not a rule: run rulesteward fapolicyd trust on the same input
allow perm=execute exe=/usr/bin/bash : path=/usr/bin/coreutils
allow perm=open exe=/usr/bin/bash : path=/usr/bin/coreutils
allow perm=open exe=/usr/bin/bash : path=/tmp/absent
--- trust exit 0 ---
# rulesteward: line 20: no trust= in this record and the path cannot be written in a rule (space or colon), so a trust entry is the only representable suggestion
# rulesteward: line 21: path is unrepresentable in a rule (space or colon; the rule parser has no quoting and a colon flips the format), and the file is already trusted, so there is nothing safe to emit
# rulesteward: line 22: trust attribute unavailable (trust=?); emitting nothing, because the file's trust state is unknown rather than untrusted
# rulesteward: 3 denial(s) need a rule, not a trust entry: run rulesteward fapolicyd rules on the same input
fapolicyd-cli --file add '/tmp/with space'
fapolicyd-cli --update
--- stderr ---
